WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.456
A driving model its README says an agent wrote, trained on

00:00:03.456 --> 00:00:04.531
labels no human drew.

00:00:04.531 --> 00:00:07.487
What does it see, and how does it pick a path?

00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:11.540
Hi, I'm Aldous!

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.087
Meteor is a driving network from Tier Four.

00:00:14.087 --> 00:00:16.952
Let's follow eight camera images to a planned path.

00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:19.018
Chapter one.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.287
Two ways to drive.

00:00:24.358 --> 00:00:26.558
The classic stack is a chain of modules.

00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:29.583
Each arrow is a message a human designed, and anything not

00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:30.683
in it is lost.

00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:34.696
Meteor is one network: cameras and speed in, a path out.

00:00:34.696 --> 00:00:38.616
But it keeps depth, boxes and lanes as outputs you can inspect.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.847
The video from the post.

00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:46.519
Eight cameras with predicted segmentation and boxes, and predicted depth for every view.

00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:51.138
On the right, the bird's-eye map: road, lanes, other cars and the planned path.

00:00:51.138 --> 00:00:52.638
All from one forward pass.

00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:55.158
Chapter two.

00:00:55.158 --> 00:00:56.458
Cameras into a grid.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.141
Lay a flat grid on the ground around the car.

00:01:03.141 --> 00:01:05.495
Meteor's is eight hundred by five hundred cells.

00:01:05.495 --> 00:01:06.377
Here, a toy.

00:01:06.797 --> 00:01:10.511
One pixel's ray crosses many cells, so plain projection smears a parked

00:01:10.511 --> 00:01:12.369
car down the road behind it.

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:16.516
So the network also predicts depth: for every pixel, a probability

00:01:16.516 --> 00:01:17.871
over sixty-four distance bins.

00:01:17.871 --> 00:01:19.904
Here it peaks at the car.

00:01:20.324 --> 00:01:24.151
Each cell takes the feature weighted by that probability, plus a small floor.

00:01:24.151 --> 00:01:25.917
Only the car's cell keeps it.

00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:27.653
A side camera agrees.

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:31.601
The grid stores the weighted average across all eight cameras, with no

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:33.247
learned weights in this step.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.860
Every grid point sits at height zero.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.539
The lift assumes a flat world.

00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:42.531
The weight is the probability that the surface lies at this cell's range.

00:01:42.951 --> 00:01:43.997
Plus a small floor.

00:01:47.842 --> 00:01:53.794
The project's diagram: one shared ResNet backbone, feeding segmentation, detection and depth heads.

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:55.985
Depth and context meet in the lift.

00:01:56.614 --> 00:02:00.013
Every bird's-eye task is a head on that grid, including the planner.

00:02:01.717 --> 00:02:02.423
Chapter three.

00:02:02.423 --> 00:02:03.483
A small planner.

00:02:07.842 --> 00:02:11.597
Strided convolutions shrink the grid and average it to one vector.

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:13.645
An MLP adds the car's speed.

00:02:14.065 --> 00:02:19.066
Out come three candidate paths, six waypoints over three seconds, each with a confidence.

00:02:19.066 --> 00:02:21.924
A later attention pool adds a small correction.

00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:25.473
Three paths, so a fork does not average into the median.

00:02:25.473 --> 00:02:27.748
The most confident one goes to a guardrail.

00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:32.871
The guardrail checks it against the network's own boxes, forecasts, traffic light and lanes.

00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:35.558
A failure vetoes it and stops in lane.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.956
Counting the released checkpoint, the planner is about eight percent of the weights.

00:02:40.956 --> 00:02:42.458
The rest exists to see.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:45.883
Chapter four.

00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:47.132
Labels and agents.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.718
Twelve heads need twelve kinds of ground truth.

00:02:54.718 --> 00:02:57.285
A pipeline makes it all from recorded drives.

00:02:57.705 --> 00:03:00.568
The planner's target is where the car actually went next.

00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:06.262
Lanes come from LiDAR points coloured by segmentation masks, accumulated over the whole drive.

00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:09.561
A LiDAR box is kept only if a camera confirms it.

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:13.686
Bad weather is re-rendered from real scenes, labels intact.

00:03:13.686 --> 00:03:16.568
No human labels, but not self-supervised either.

00:03:18.383 --> 00:03:22.578
The factory's output for one scene, every label generated from the recording.

00:03:24.758 --> 00:03:28.369
Per the README, Claude wrote the code and ran training in

00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:29.682
rounds, each judged automatically.

00:03:29.682 --> 00:03:33.292
For two months, a cache bug fed the planner zero history.

00:03:33.712 --> 00:03:37.681
With real history, trajectories got thirty-three to thirty-eight percent worse.

00:03:38.101 --> 00:03:39.746
So the release is single-frame.

00:03:39.746 --> 00:03:42.377
The judge never checked that input was non-zero.

00:03:44.508 --> 00:03:45.065
Chapter five.

00:03:45.065 --> 00:03:46.179
Does it hold up?

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.205
The post said sixteen million parameters.

00:03:52.205 --> 00:03:54.609
The released checkpoint has fifty-four million.

00:03:58.383 --> 00:04:03.191
Its comparison, Auto E to E, plans in green over the human's purple path.

00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:06.969
Both are scored open loop, over footage the plan never changes.

00:04:08.233 --> 00:04:12.345
Its closed-loop test chains plans over logged images that never react.

00:04:12.765 --> 00:04:15.065
The deployed model sees one frame at a time.

00:04:15.485 --> 00:04:18.948
It was trained on one sensor rig, mostly on Japanese roads.

00:04:19.368 --> 00:04:23.278
And its model card says it must not control a vehicle on public roads.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:30.029
Meteor is a strong open reference: big perception, a small planner, a rule checker.

00:04:30.029 --> 00:04:31.879
Open-source FSD it is not.

00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:34.889
Depth decides where features land.

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:36.213
The planner is small.

00:04:36.213 --> 00:04:38.862
And the labels come from machines, not people.

00:04:39.282 --> 00:04:41.391
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:41.391 --> 00:04:41.994
I'm Aldous.

00:04:41.994 --> 00:04:42.295
Bye!
